 can squash  out those compounds that we  for in quality wine and overall water  scarcity across the board so not just in  the culture but all agricultural  pursuits are gonna be dealing with water  scarcity so what can we do because in  the coming decades I want to be like a  world-famous viticulturist so I have to  deal with this right so we have to do  something and even if we stop emitting  carbon today right now even if we shut  off all our cars and stop eating beef  we're still going to be dealing with the  effects of climate change in the years  to come because the climate has inertia  so at this point the best risk  management strategy is adaptation so  here's a graph that I put together of  the historical occurrence of heat waves  up in the Riverland which was that  northeastern yellow area that I  discussed really really hot high  intensity agriculture high yields and  last year 1617 we only had four heat  waves so that's relatively fewer than in  previous years but we only expect that  trend to increase as the years go by so  how do we define a heat wave for our  study for this purpose we define a heat  wave as three days or more of 35 degrees  Celsius or greater and we arrived at  that definition using the Greer and  Whedon paper where they showed that at  that threshold we're seeing negative  effects on vine physiology and wine  quality great ventolin qualities as far  as active canopy cooling goes the whole  point of this presentation there's not  too much in the field on active canopy  cooling in the 70s in California Algebra  II and Gilbert looked at overhead  sprinkling and they did have some  success with that but it hasn't really  been touched since more recently last  year curuvija at the University of  Adelaide investigated in canopy cooling  so misting in the canopy and we actually  have an extension of that project  happening this year that I worked on as  well I'm not going to go into that one  in depth for this presentation but I'm  happy to talk about it with any of you  if you're curious later  so our objectives for this work were to  evaluate the vine performance during the  heat waves we looked at the canopy  microclimate so the temperature the  humidity the vapor pressure deficit and  we looked at the effects on grape and  wine quality so we were up in the  Riverland as I said we were working with  a commercial grower on Savion Blanc  vines for court on sprawl absolutely  huge vines very high yielding and we had  these two trials at the same site right  next to each other the in canopy cooling  trial which was a misting in the canopy  trial that I just mentioned but I'll  talk about later if you want and then  the under canopy cooling trial which is  a special interest to me and that's what  we'll talk about today so the under  canopy cooling trial or UCC had two  treatments compared to control the  control was your traditional drip line  irrigation grower controlled just your  regular drip line the under canopy  sprinklers the first treatment or UCS  were basically broadcast sprinklers that  wet the entire mid row of the vineyard  floor so under the vine in the middle of  the vine everywhere was wet and then the  supplemental irrigation or si was a  bypass drip line that deposited the same  volume of water but directly under the  vine instead of everywhere like the UCS  did it put the same amount of water just  under the vine oh sorry and the most  important thing about these treatments  is to keep in mind that they were on at  night so if we saw a heat wave was  coming up we would trigger the system to  go on the night before and all of the  nights during the heat wave but this  wasn't happening during the day so we  collected a lot of data because Vinay  loves data and I am going to primarily  talk to you about the data we collected  on the caffeine microclimate the leaves  gas exchange and the soil and plant  water statuses but don't worry we also  looked at a lot of other things so that  woul